# First in India: The Trailblazers Every Exam Asks About

> Every republic keeps a shelf of firsts. India's shelf is unusually
> crowded and unusually examined: the first President, the first woman
> in space, the first Indian Nobel laureate, the first film with sound.
> These questions look like memory tests, and they are — but the
> underlying stories trace how fast a colonised society built modern
> institutions of its own.

## 1. Firsts of the Republic — President, Prime Minister, and the Rest

| Office | First holder | First woman |
| --- | --- | --- |
| President | Dr. Rajendra Prasad (1950–62) | Pratibha Patil (2007) |
| Prime Minister | Jawaharlal Nehru (1947–64) | Indira Gandhi (1966) |
| Vice President | Dr. S. Radhakrishnan | — |
| Speaker of Lok Sabha | G.V. Mavalankar | Meira Kumar (2009) |
| Chief Justice of India | H.J. Kania | Fathima Beevi (1989, Supreme Court judge) |
| Governor-General (free India) | Lord Mountbatten | — |
| Governor-General (Indian) | C. Rajagopalachari | — |
| Chief Minister of a state | — | Sucheta Kripalani (UP, 1963) |
| Home Minister / "Iron Man" | Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel | — |
| Education Minister | Maulana Abul Kalam Azad | — |

[definition]
**Dr. Rajendra Prasad** holds two records at once: he was the first
President of India and the only President to serve **three terms**
(elected 1952, 1957, and continuing from 1950 as provisional head
until the first election).
[/definition]

The Mountbatten-to-Rajagopalachari sequence is a classic exam pair:
the last British Governor-General was **Lord Mountbatten** (1947–48),
and the first — and only — Indian Governor-General was **C.
Rajagopalachari** (1948–50), after which the office was replaced by
the Presidency.

## 2. Firsts in Science, Space, and Medicine

| First | Person / Event | Year |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Indian to win a Nobel Prize | Rabindranath Tagore (Literature) | 1913 |
| Indian Nobel in the sciences | C.V. Raman (Physics, Raman effect) | 1930 |
| Indian in space | Rakesh Sharma (Soyuz T-11) | 1984 |
| Woman of Indian origin in space | Kalpana Chawla (Columbia) | 1997 |
| Indian woman in the IFS | Chonira Belliappa Muthamma | 1949 |
| Indian woman doctor | Anandi Gopal Joshi (MD, Women's Medical College, Philadelphia) | 1886 |
| Indian to pass the ICS | Satyendranath Tagore | 1863 |
| Indian woman IAS topper / officer | Anna Rajam Malhotra (first woman IAS officer, 1951 batch) | 1951 |
| First Indian woman IPS officer | Kiran Bedi | 1972 |
| Test-tube baby (India) | "Durga" (Kanupriya Agarwal), under Dr. Subhash Mukhopadhyay | 1978 |
| Nuclear test | "Smiling Buddha", Pokhran | 1974 |
| Satellite | Aryabhata | 1975 |
| Indian to reach Mt. Everest | Tenzing Norgay (with Edmund Hillary) | 1953 |
| Indian woman on Everest | Bachendri Pal | 1984 |

Two clarifications papers like to hide in options: Rakesh Sharma flew
as a **cosmonaut on a Soviet spacecraft**, answering the famous
question from space — "How does India look from above?" — with
"Saare Jahan Se Achha." Kalpana Chawla, though of Indian birth, flew
as a **US citizen**; the trap option "first Indian woman in space"
belongs to no one until an Indian-flagged crewed mission flies.

Subhash Mukhopadhyay's test-tube achievement arrived barely two
months after the world's first (Louise Brown, July 1978) and was
recognised only posthumously — a bitter arc the exam reduces to the
year 1978.

## 3. Firsts in Arts, Sports, and Civil Society

| First | Person | Year |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Bharat Ratna (jointly, three recipients) | C. Rajagopalachari, S. Radhakrishnan, C.V. Raman | 1954 |
| Dadasaheb Phalke Award | Devika Rani | 1969 |
| Indian sound film | *Alam Ara* (dir. Ardeshir Irani) | 1931 |
| Miss World (from India) | Reita Faria | 1966 |
| Miss Universe (from India) | Sushmita Sen | 1994 |
| Chess Grandmaster (India) | Viswanathan Anand | 1988 |
| Individual Olympic gold | Abhinav Bindra (10 m air rifle shooting) | 2008 |
| Nobel Peace Prize (Indian citizen) | Mother Teresa | 1979 |
| Booker Prize (Indian novelist) | Arundhati Roy, *The God of Small Things* | 1997 |
| Oscar winner (India-born) | Bhanu Athaiya (costume design, *Gandhi*) | 1983 |
| First Field Marshal | Sam Manekshaw | 1973 |
| First Marshal of the Air Force | Arjan Singh | 2002 |

The sporting line matters for its fine print: India won Olympic hockey
golds from 1928 onward, but those were **team** medals. The first
**individual** medal of independent India was K.D. Jadhav's wrestling
bronze (1952), and the first individual **gold** came only in 2008
with Abhinav Bindra.

## 4. Firsts in Law, Letters, and Literature

The first woman judge of a High Court was **Anna Chandy** (Kerala,
1959); the first woman judge of the Supreme Court was **Justice Fathima
Beevi** (1989). The first woman Chief Election Commissioner's chair is
a trap — it has never been held by a woman as of recent years, though
Sushma Swaraj became the first woman **Leader of the Opposition**
(Lok Sabha) in 2009, and the first woman Speaker, **Meira Kumar**,
took office the same year.

In literature, Bankim Chandra Chatterjee's *Rajmohan's Wife* (1864)
is counted the first Indian English novel, while Toru Dutt is
remembered as the first Indian woman to publish a novel in English and
a pioneer of Indo-Anglian poetry in the 1870s — decades before Tagore
carried Indian writing to the world's stage.

[example]
An exam asks: "Who was the first woman President of India?" with
Indira Gandhi as the trap option. Indira Gandhi was the first woman
**Prime Minister** (1966); the Presidency waited until Pratibha Patil
in 2007. The same paper inverts it: "first woman Prime Minister" —
now the trap is Pratibha Patil. Pair-traps like this decide marks;
keep the two ladders separate in memory.
[/example]

[summary]
The exam-stable of Indian firsts: Rajendra Prasad (first President,
three terms), Nehru (first PM), Indira Gandhi (first woman PM),
Pratibha Patil (first woman President), Mountbatten then C.
Rajagopalachari as the last two Governors-General, Tagore (first
Indian Nobel, 1913), C.V. Raman (first science Nobel, 1930), Rakesh
Sharma (first Indian in space, 1984), Anandi Gopal Joshi (first woman
doctor), Kiran Bedi (first woman IPS), Bachendri Pal (first Indian
woman on Everest, 1984), *Alam Ara* (first sound film, 1931),
Viswanathan Anand (first Grandmaster, 1988) and Abhinav Bindra (first
individual Olympic gold, 2008). Wherever two records sit close
together — PM vs President, gold vs individual medal — expect the
paper to swap them.
[/summary]
